1
00:00:00,758 --> 00:00:05,607
This is the DOGFOOD portal: self-hostable
hackathon submission and judging,

2
00:00:05,607 --> 00:00:08,840
with results that anyone can
reproduce and verify,

3
00:00:08,840 --> 00:00:11,491
without trusting whoever runs the server.

4
00:00:12,095 --> 00:00:16,997
In the next few minutes, we'll run one full
event lifecycle on a live portal.

5
00:00:16,997 --> 00:00:21,007
The organizer creates an event, a team
submits, a judge scores,

6
00:00:21,007 --> 00:00:25,399
the organizer publishes, and a standalone
verifier proves the result.

7
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:30,664
It's one container, and it runs fully
offline. On first boot, it migrates,

8
00:00:30,664 --> 00:00:35,076
loads the shared fixtures, and creates one
seed account for each role.

9
00:00:35,677 --> 00:00:39,298
The health check answers, and forty-one
fixture projects are ready.

10
00:00:40,302 --> 00:00:41,514
Step one: create.

11
00:00:43,418 --> 00:00:47,691
Here's the public home page. The
organizer console is one click away.

12
00:00:49,841 --> 00:00:52,467
The organizer signs in with the seed login.

13
00:00:55,204 --> 00:00:57,942
Every core table is browsable
from the console.

14
00:00:58,543 --> 00:01:01,169
We create a new event, DOGFOOD Demo Finals,

15
00:01:01,169 --> 00:01:04,345
with its submission deadline at
the end of December.

16
00:01:12,473 --> 00:01:17,526
Saved. It sits right alongside the fixture
event, Sample Hack twenty twenty-six.

17
00:01:18,128 --> 00:01:20,643
Next, a team for the new event: Demo Crew.

18
00:01:24,834 --> 00:01:29,294
And the rubric. Each criterion carries a
weight the organizer controls:

19
00:01:29,294 --> 00:01:34,445
forty percent functionality, forty percent
quality, and twenty percent innovation.

20
00:01:35,050 --> 00:01:37,537
These weights ship inside the signed results,

21
00:01:37,537 --> 00:01:40,576
so anyone can see exactly how the
scores were combined.

22
00:01:41,180 --> 00:01:42,478
Step two: submit.

23
00:01:44,282 --> 00:01:47,602
Projects land in a public
gallery. No login needed.

24
00:01:48,204 --> 00:01:50,189
Search covers titles and summaries.

25
00:01:51,455 --> 00:01:55,519
Deadlines are enforced by the back
end, not by hiding a button.

26
00:01:55,519 --> 00:01:57,325
Sample Hack closed in March,

27
00:01:57,325 --> 00:02:01,130
so a participant posting a late
entry gets a four-oh-three.

28
00:02:02,032 --> 00:02:04,428
And the refusal is written to the audit log.

29
00:02:05,029 --> 00:02:09,390
In this build, a new entry is recorded
through the organizer console.

30
00:02:09,390 --> 00:02:13,498
Demo Lantern goes in for Demo Crew, on
the developer tools track.

31
00:02:18,677 --> 00:02:20,146
And it's marked as submitted.

32
00:02:27,236 --> 00:02:29,668
And there it is, live in the public gallery.

33
00:02:30,271 --> 00:02:31,559
Step three: judge.

34
00:02:33,361 --> 00:02:37,065
Judge oh-seven scores Demo Lantern
on the weighted rubric:

35
00:02:37,065 --> 00:02:41,088
fives for functionality and quality,
and a four for innovation.

36
00:02:54,396 --> 00:02:55,336
Score saved.

37
00:02:55,938 --> 00:03:01,439
Role isolation lives in the API. Judge
oh-seven sees only their own scores,

38
00:03:01,439 --> 00:03:03,126
including Demo Lantern.

39
00:03:03,727 --> 00:03:08,682
When another judge asks for those scores, the
API refuses with a four-oh-three.

40
00:03:08,682 --> 00:03:10,062
So does a participant.

41
00:03:10,663 --> 00:03:14,904
The organizer, meanwhile, sees judging
progress across the whole event.

42
00:03:16,006 --> 00:03:20,784
Now the unmodified DOGFOOD acceptance
checker, run against this live portal.

43
00:03:21,385 --> 00:03:23,003
Seven of seven checks pass.

44
00:03:23,907 --> 00:03:25,194
Step four: publish.

45
00:03:26,998 --> 00:03:31,475
Before publishing, the public sees nothing.
There's no results bundle yet,

46
00:03:31,475 --> 00:03:34,136
and a judge who tries to publish is refused.

47
00:03:34,738 --> 00:03:40,514
Only the organizer can publish, and publishing
and signing are a single action.

48
00:03:40,514 --> 00:03:45,412
Back comes a signed bundle, with its
digest, the Ed25519 signature,

49
00:03:45,412 --> 00:03:47,313
and the exact code commit.

50
00:03:47,915 --> 00:03:53,476
The ranking is now public, with scores
adjusted for harsh and generous judges,

51
00:03:53,476 --> 00:03:55,472
as documented in JUDGING.md.

52
00:03:56,073 --> 00:03:59,555
The organizer can also export
the results as CSV.

53
00:04:00,559 --> 00:04:02,000
Step five: verify.

54
00:04:03,803 --> 00:04:08,502
Anyone can download the bundle. It carries
every raw score, the rubric weights,

55
00:04:08,502 --> 00:04:12,308
and the method, along with the portal's
public verification key.

56
00:04:12,910 --> 00:04:17,044
The standalone verifier recomputes the
ranking from the raw scores,

57
00:04:17,044 --> 00:04:19,883
checks the digest, and verifies the signature.

58
00:04:20,484 --> 00:04:21,290
Verified.

59
00:04:21,891 --> 00:04:26,142
Now, let's tamper with a single judge's
score, and bump it up to all fives.

60
00:04:26,743 --> 00:04:29,716
Run the verifier again, and it
refuses the bundle.

61
00:04:30,316 --> 00:04:31,294
Not verified.

62
00:04:32,296 --> 00:04:36,512
Create, submit, judge, publish,
verify. One container, offline,

63
00:04:36,512 --> 00:04:40,461
seeded from the shared fixtures.
Roles enforced in the API,

64
00:04:40,461 --> 00:04:45,749
and a winner that anyone can prove from the
signed bundle. Thanks for watching.

